distance":0.7992,"shared":18,"play_caller":"DeMeco Ryans"},{"season":2023,"team":"SF","distance":0.8085,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"NYJ","distance":0.835,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Jeff Ulbrich"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Texans went 10-7 in 2023 and outscored opponents 377 to 353. The offense ranked 15th in EPA per play and the defense 13th in EPA allowed, so neither side stood out much. What set them apart was the 2nd lowest RPO rate and the highest Cover 6 rate in the league.","offense":"The Texans offense was about average by EPA per play, and it was much better passing the ball than running it. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the 2nd lowest RPO rate in the league. C.J. Stroud ran the passing game, with Nico Collins leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Texans defense was a little above average by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the run than the pass, ranking 5th and 19th. Jerry Hughes was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Jonathan Greenard, who had 12.5 sacks, after doing little as a pass rusher a year ago. Schematically, they had the highest Cover 6 rate in the league.","special":"The Texans were one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 17% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers made 89% of field goals, which was a little above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the offensive line, about 28% of the money on the books. The offense also turned over more of its roster than most, with 23% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"C.J. Stroud played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 4,108 yards. Targets were spread around, with Nico Collins and Dalton Schultz leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield was a committee led by Devin Singletary and Dameon Pierce. On defense, Jonathan Greenard led the team with 12.5 sacks and Derek Stingley Jr. picked off 5 passes.","coaching":"Bobby Slowik called the offense and DeMeco Ryans called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2023 and 2022 Raiders. Their defense looked most like the 2025 49ers under Robert Saleh and the 2018 Raiders.","trends":"The offense went from 32nd in EPA per play in 2022 to 15th in 2023, a big step forward. The defense moved from 17th in EPA allowed per play in 2022 to 13th in 2023, a modest improvement. The biggest change in approach was Cover 2 rate, which went from 32% to 11%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2023. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}